### What is a Trie?

A Trie (prefix tree) is a tree data structure where each edge represents one character. Every path from the root spells a prefix of a stored word. A boolean end-of-word flag on a node marks that the prefix up to that node is a complete word. This structure allows insert, exact search, and prefix search in time proportional to the key length.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class Trie {
    private TrieNode root = new TrieNode();

    public void insert(String word) {
        TrieNode node = root;
        for (char c : word.toCharArray()) {
            node = node.children.computeIfAbsent(c, k -> new TrieNode());
        }
        node.isWord = true;
    }

    public boolean search(String word) {
        TrieNode node = traverse(word);
        return node != null && node.isWord;
    }

    public boolean startsWith(String prefix) {
        return traverse(prefix) != null;
    }

    private TrieNode traverse(String key) {
        TrieNode node = root;
        for (char c : key.toCharArray()) {
            node = node.children.get(c);
            if (node == null) return null;
        }
        return node;
    }
}

class TrieNode {
    Map<Character, TrieNode> children = new HashMap<>();
    boolean isWord = false;
}
```

### How does insert work?

Insert walks the word character by character from the root. At each character, it either follows an existing child or creates a new one using `computeIfAbsent`. After the last character, it sets the end-of-word flag to true.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class InsertDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Trie trie = new Trie();
        trie.insert("ICIC");
        trie.insert("ICICI");
        System.out.println(trie.search("ICIC"));     // true
        System.out.println(trie.search("ICICI"));    // true
        System.out.println(trie.search("ICI"));      // false (prefix, not word)
        System.out.println(trie.startsWith("ICI"));  // true
    }
}
```

```text
true
true
false
true
```

### How is search different from startsWith?

Both operations traverse the Trie identically. The only difference is the final check. Search requires the end-of-word flag to be true at the last node. startsWith only requires that the path exists, regardless of the flag. This is the key distinction interviewers look for.

```java
// search: path must exist AND isWord must be true
public boolean search(String word) {
    TrieNode node = traverse(word);
    return node != null && node.isWord;  // both conditions
}

// startsWith: path must exist (flag ignored)
public boolean startsWith(String prefix) {
    return traverse(prefix) != null;     // only path existence
}
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

Each operation traverses at most L characters, where L is the key length. The time is independent of how many words are stored, which is the Trie's main advantage over hash-based lookups for prefix queries.

- **Time** – O(L) per operation where L is the key length.
- **Space** – O(total characters across all inserted words) in the worst case.
- **Comparison** – a HashSet gives O(L) search but cannot answer prefix queries without scanning all keys.

```java
// Time per operation: O(L) where L = key length
// Independent of N (number of stored words)
// HashSet.contains("ICICI"): O(L) for hashing, but
// HashSet has no startsWith operation at all.
```

### What is the HashMap vs fixed-array trade-off?

Using a HashMap for children handles any alphabet flexibly but has higher memory overhead and pointer chasing. A fixed array of 26 (for lowercase letters) is faster and more cache-friendly but wastes space when the alphabet is sparse.

| Aspect | HashMap Children | Fixed Array [26] |
|---|---|---|
| Alphabet | Any characters | Lowercase a-z only |
| Memory | Proportional to used children | 26 slots per node (wasteful if sparse) |
| Speed | Hash lookups, pointer chasing | Direct index, cache-friendly |
| Flexibility | High | Low |

```java
// HashMap version
class TrieNode {
    Map<Character, TrieNode> children = new HashMap<>();
    boolean isWord;
}

// Fixed array version (lowercase only)
class TrieNodeArray {
    TrieNodeArray[] children = new TrieNodeArray[26];
    boolean isWord;
    // Access: children[c - 'a']
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include searching for an empty string, inserting the same word twice, and searching for a word that is a prefix of another stored word.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class TrieEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Trie trie = new Trie();

        // Insert and search same word twice
        trie.insert("HDFC");
        trie.insert("HDFC");
        System.out.println(trie.search("HDFC")); // true

        // Word is prefix of another
        trie.insert("HDFCBANK");
        System.out.println(trie.search("HDFC"));     // true (inserted separately)
        System.out.println(trie.search("HDFCBANK")); // true
        System.out.println(trie.startsWith("HDF"));  // true
    }
}
```

```text
true
true
true
true
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting the end-of-word flag, causing search to return true for any prefix of a stored word. Another mistake is not returning false when a character is not found during traversal.

```java
// WRONG: no isWord check in search
public boolean searchWrong(String word) {
    TrieNode node = traverse(word);
    return node != null; // returns true for prefixes too!
}

// CORRECT: check isWord
public boolean searchCorrect(String word) {
    TrieNode node = traverse(word);
    return node != null && node.isWord;
}
```

### How would you apply this in a banking system?

At the National Bank of India, a Trie powers type-ahead for IFSC branch codes on the banking portal. Each keystroke calls startsWith to offer suggestions and search to determine whether the typed string is a complete valid code. Because the directory holds hundreds of thousands of codes, a per-keystroke cost proportional to the directory size would be unacceptable.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class IFSCTypeAheadService {
    private final Trie trie = new Trie();

    public void loadCodes(List<String> ifscCodes) {
        for (String code : ifscCodes) trie.insert(code);
    }

    public boolean isValidCode(String code) {
        return trie.search(code);
    }

    public boolean hasCodesStartingWith(String prefix) {
        return trie.startsWith(prefix);
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know that Tries are the basis for autocomplete, spell-check, IP routing (longest prefix match), and T9 predictive text. They should also know about compressed Tries (radix trees) that merge chains of single-child nodes to save space, and concurrent Tries for thread-safe lookups.

```java
// Trie applications:
// 1. Autocomplete / type-ahead suggestions
// 2. Spell checking (edit-distance search)
// 3. IP routing (longest prefix match)
// 4. T9 predictive text input
// 5. Word games (Boggle, Scrabble)

// Compressed Trie (radix tree):
// Merges single-child chains: a->p->p->l->e becomes "apple" edge
// Saves space for sparse Tries
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers often ask follow-ups that build on the basic Trie:

- Add a delete operation that removes a word and cleans up orphan nodes.
- Add a method to list all words with a given prefix (DFS from the prefix node).
- Design a word search where dots match any character (wildcard search with backtracking).

```java
// Follow-up: find all words with prefix
public List<String> findAllWithPrefix(String prefix) {
    List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
    TrieNode node = traverse(prefix);
    if (node != null) dfs(node, new StringBuilder(prefix), result);
    return result;
}

private void dfs(TrieNode node, StringBuilder path, List<String> result) {
    if (node.isWord) result.add(path.toString());
    for (var entry : node.children.entrySet()) {
        path.append(entry.getKey());
        dfs(entry.getValue(), path, result);
        path.deleteCharAt(path.length() - 1);
    }
}
```
